  <h1>India’s Electoral Reforms — Free, Fair, and Inclusive Democracy</h1>
  <div><em>Indian Express | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India, the world’s largest democracy, prides itself on regular and peaceful elections. Yet, debates on electoral reforms remain central to improving transparency, fairness, and inclusivity. In 2024, Indian Express analysed proposals around electoral bonds, criminalisation, and technology in elections. For UPSC, this is a crucial topic in polity, governance, and ethics.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Voter Base (2024): ~96 crore eligible voters.<br/>•	Turnout: ~67% in General Elections 2019, higher than many democracies.<br/>•	Criminalisation: ~43% MPs with criminal cases (ADR data).<br/>•	Money Power: Election expenditure estimated at $8–9 billion (2019).<br/>•	Technology: 100% EVM use, expanding VVPAT verification.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Key Issues<br/>1.	Money Power: Electoral bonds criticised for opacity.<br/>2.	Criminalisation: Rising number of legislators with pending criminal cases.<br/>3.	Voter Awareness: Low civic literacy, misinformation.<br/>4.	Fragmented Regulation: Overlap between EC, judiciary, and Parliament.<br/>5.	Inclusion Gaps: Migrant workers, disabled persons face barriers.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Reform Measures Discussed<br/>•	Electoral Bonds Scrutiny: SC hearings questioning transparency.<br/>•	State Funding of Elections: Proposed by several commissions.<br/>•	Disqualification Rules: Stricter provisions against criminal candidates.<br/>•	Simultaneous Elections (“One Nation, One Election”): Debated for efficiency.<br/>•	Technology Integration: Blockchain voting pilots considered.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Recommendations from Committees<br/>•	Law Commission (2015): Ban on convicted candidates, regulate inner-party democracy.<br/>•	ECI Proposals:<br/>o	Permanent ban on convicted persons.<br/>o	Cap on anonymous donations at lower levels.<br/>•	Second ARC (2008): Transparent funding, strengthening EC autonomy.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	High Voter Turnout: Women’s participation almost equal to men.<br/>•	Tech Reforms: EVMs + VVPAT increase trust.<br/>•	Awareness Drives: SVEEP programs improve registration and turnout.<br/>•	Global Recognition: India conducts largest elections smoothly.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>•	Opaque Funding: Electoral bonds system undermines transparency.<br/>•	Weak Enforcement: EC lacks full powers to debar candidates.<br/>•	Populism: Freebies and competitive populism distort democracy.<br/>•	Digital Threats: Social media misinformation, deepfakes.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Democratic Legitimacy: Free and fair elections strengthen trust.<br/>•	Political Equality: Reducing money and muscle power ensures level playing field.<br/>•	Governance Quality: Clean candidates improve policymaking.<br/>•	Global Standing: India as model democracy for Global South.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Polity): Electoral reforms, ECI.<br/>•	GS-IV (Ethics): Political morality, transparency.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Elections are the festival of democracy, but reforms are its oxygen.”<br/>o	“Democracy is only as strong as the integrity of its elections.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Institutions and democratic deepening.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Transparent Funding: Replace electoral bonds with real-time public disclosures.<br/>2.	Decriminalisation: Ban candidates with serious criminal charges.<br/>3.	State Support: Partial public funding of elections.<br/>4.	Inclusive Voting: Remote voting for migrants, disabled-friendly polling.<br/>5.	Empower EC: Give constitutional status to EC’s recommendations.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s electoral system is robust yet requires continuous reforms to preserve credibility. Ensuring transparency, accountability, and inclusivity will keep democracy resilient. For UPSC aspirants, electoral reforms illustrate how constitutional ideals and political practices interact in Indian governance.</p>
